\alias{run_scenario}
\title{Define and run replicated scenarios}
\usage{
scenario_spec(
  name,
  n_runs,
  n_steps,
  init_fun,
  processes,
  seed = NULL,
  dt = 1,
  initial_census = NULL
)

run_scenario(
  scenario,
  parallel = FALSE,
  workers = 2L,
  backend = c("auto", "fork", "psock"),
  cluster = NULL,
  validation = c("strict", "fast"),
  keep = c("census", "events", "accounting")
)
}
\arguments{
\item{name}{Scenario name.}
\item{n_runs}{Number of Monte Carlo replicates.}
\item{n_steps}{Number of lifecycle intervals per replicate.}
\item{init_fun}{Function with no arguments returning a \code{pva_state} (or a population). It is called inside each replicate's random-number stream.}
\item{processes}{Ordered list of \code{pva_process} objects executed every interval.}
\item{seed}{Root seed. With \code{NULL} the caller's RNG supplies one root draw, so successive unseeded calls advance.}
\item{dt}{Interval duration supplied in the process context.}
\item{initial_census}{Optional non-mutating census process run once before the first interval.}
\item{scenario}{A scenario from \code{scenario_spec()}.}
\item{parallel}{Whether to run replicates on several local workers.}
\item{workers}{Number of local worker processes.}
\item{backend}{\code{"fork"}, \code{"psock"}, or \code{"auto"} (fork on Unix, PSOCK on Windows).}
\item{cluster}{Optional cluster from \code{parallel::makePSOCKcluster()}. Use this to reuse workers or to initialise dependencies looked up dynamically.}
\item{validation}{\code{"strict"} checks every process transition (declared writes, types, engine-owned fields and cohort accounting). \code{"fast"} skips those checks and gives bit-identical output for a valid model.}
\item{keep}{Which outputs to retain: any of \code{"census"}, \code{"events"}, \code{"accounting"}, \code{"state"} (final states) and \code{"ledger"} (every individual that ever lived, with its fate). The default retains census, events and accounting. Request final states or ledgers explicitly.}
}
\description{
\code{scenario_spec()} bundles an initial state, an ordered lifecycle and a replicate design. \code{run_scenario()} executes it serially or in parallel.
}
\details{
Each replicate receives its own L'Ecuyer-CMRG stream, assigned before any work is dispatched, so results are bit-identical for a given seed whether replicates run serially, on fork workers or on a PSOCK cluster. After the optional root draw the caller's RNG kind and seed are restored, including on error.

Managed socket workers start with \code{--vanilla}. Both runners propagate the parent library paths and export statically referenced callback helpers and data. A supplied cluster can initialise resources resolved dynamically by the model.
}
\value{
\code{scenario_spec()} returns a \code{pva_scenario}. \code{run_scenario()} returns a \code{pva_run}: a list with the scenario, data frames \code{census}, \code{events} and \code{accounting}, lists \code{states} and \code{ledgers}, and \code{provenance} (root seed, streams, validation mode, process plan and versions).
}
